    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d535">

                <pb facs="0629" n="617" xml:id="pg617"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.51 War Credits/772: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-08-07T17:00:00+02:00">August 7, 1940—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 7—4:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>190. My number 182, July 31. Not having received a reply as promised, I again saw
                    the Under Secretary for Foreign Affairs this morning, at which time he excused
                    his delay on grounds of personal illness and the difficulty of evolving as
                    satisfactory a reply as possible under the conditions now affecting the Greek
                    treasury. He said he would need several more days as well as further conference
                    with the Minister of Finance, which he arranged in my presence, adding that the
                    contents and tenor of the Greek Government’s message are of more importance than
                    its date.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
